00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:18.940
About 14 days ago we picked KO for its pricing power and it closed flat today, reminding us defensive

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:25.540
names protect capital but rarely outperform in sideways markets. A covered call ETF strategy

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:30.920
lets investors collect premium income on holdings they already own by selling out of the money calls

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:37.260
each month. Today's flat equity tape and elevated volatility make the approach attractive because

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:43.880
premiums remain rich while downside risk stays contained. Implementation is straightforward on Canadian

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:54.320
platforms. Buy an ETF such as ZWC or QYLD inside a TFSA then sell the front month call at the 30 delta

00:01:54.320 --> 00:02:01.660
strike using the brokerage's options chain. The tactic has historically added 4-8% annualized yield

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:08.260
in sideways or mildly bullish regimes. It caps upside during strong rallies and can underperform

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:15.460
in sharp drops. Position sizing matters, limit covered call exposure to 15-20% of the overall

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:22.580
portfolio so the income sleeve does not dominate total return. Track the strategy's net performance

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:28.700
against the plain vanilla equity ETF each quarter to confirm the income premium justifies the

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:35.820
opportunity cost. This is a simulated trade for educational purposes only. No real money is involved.

