00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:54.360
Our AI analysis is learning from patterns around catalyst confirmation and adjusting entry

00:06:54.360 --> 00:07:01.180
as accordingly. Imagine you bought 200 shares of a healthcare name at $480 last week

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:08.080
after an analyst upgrade. Your order filled cleanly at the open. The position now represents 18%

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:15.220
of your TFSA because you sized it by dollars rather than by risk. A 6% adverse move would wipe out

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:21.560
an entire month of expected gains. The mechanism is simple. Maximum loss equals position size

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:29.520
multiplied by distance to stop. If you risk only 1% of total capital per trade, a 6% stop requires

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:36.640
the position to be no larger than 16.7% of the account. Professionals always calculate the dollar

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:42.820
risk first, then back into share count using the planned stop level. The biggest mistake with position

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:50.660
sizing is letting a high conviction idea exceed the 1-2% risk budget. Instead, always pre-define the

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:57.020
exit price and the maximum portfolio impact before the order is placed. Before we wrap,

